  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is inside an ink cartridge?",
      "a": "A cartridge holds liquid ink, often soaked in a sponge or kept in a bag so it does not leak. Some cartridges include the print head nozzles themselves, so you get a new head with every refill.",
      "asks": [
        "How do ink cartridges work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does an inkjet nozzle fire a drop?",
      "a": "In a thermal printer, a tiny resistor heats the ink to boiling in microseconds, making a bubble that shoots a drop out of the nozzle. In a piezo printer, a crystal flexes when voltage is applied and squeezes the drop out.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a print head work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does the print head move across the page?",
      "a": "The head rides on a rail and is pulled by a toothed belt driven by a motor. A strip with fine markings lets the printer know exactly where the head is, so each drop lands in the right place.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the printer head go back and forth?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does paper move through a printer?",
      "a": "Rubber rollers grip the paper and a motor turns them by tiny, exact amounts after each pass of the head, so the printed stripes line up with no gaps.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does paper get stuck in a printer?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a printer use only four colours of ink?",
      "a": "Cyan, magenta and yellow can be mixed in tiny dot patterns to make almost any colour, and black gives crisp text and deep shadows. Your eye blends the dots, which are too small to see separately.",
      "asks": [
        "What does CMYK mean?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Thermal inkjet printing was developed independently by Canon, as Bubble Jet, and Hewlett-Packard in the late 1970s and early 1980s.",
    "Inkjet drops can be just a few picolitres, trillionths of a litre."
  ],
